# Cache headers for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# A plain text file called `_headers`, no extension, in the site root. Pages
# reads it and overrides the defaults it would otherwise send.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# Pages already defaults to `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is
# safe: the browser checks with the server before reusing anything. So the
# site was never going to serve a stale build for long.
#
# But it means EVERY file is revalidated on EVERY load. Seventy-two image
# sheets, forty-odd scripts, the audio — that is a round trip each, and on a
# phone in Berlin the round trips are the slow part, not the bytes.
#
# Now that every asset URL carries `?v=NNN` — scripts and stylesheets from
# index.html, images, faces and audio from js/build.js — a changed file is a
# changed URL. So they can be cached hard and never revalidated, and a deploy
# still reaches her immediately.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE FILE THAT MUST NOT BE CACHED
#
# index.html is what names all the other versions. If it is stale, every
# version it names is stale too, and nothing else can rescue that. It is the
# single point where a bad cache rule breaks everything, which is why it gets
# an explicit rule rather than relying on a default.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IF THIS IS EVER HOSTED SOMEWHERE ELSE
#
# Netlify reads the same file and the same syntax. Anything else — nginx,
# Apache, GitHub Pages — needs its own equivalent, and GitHub Pages in
# particular gives no control over headers at all. The defaults there cache
# HTML for ten minutes, which is survivable but not what this describes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# index.html — always revalidate. Everything depends on this being fresh.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Versioned assets. `immutable` tells the browser not to revalidate even on
# an explicit refresh, which is only safe BECAUSE the URL changes when the
# file does. One year, which is the conventional maximum.
/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/audio/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
